    "details" : {
      "en" : {
        "id" : "en",
        "purpose" : "To predict 30-day mortality in patients with pulmonary embolism, and help distinguish between low-risk individuals who are potential candidates for out-patient care and high-risk patients who require high dependency/intensive care.",
        "keywords" : [
          "pesi",
          "pulmonary",
          "embolism",
          "severity",
          "index",
          "thromboembolism",
          "mortality",
          "risk",
          "stratification"
        ],
        "use" : "To calculate the pulmonary embolism severity index using the following 11 variables:\n- demographic characteristics (age = +age in years; male sex = +10)\n- comorbidities (cancer = +30; heart failure = +10; chronic lung disease = +10)\n- clinical findings (pulse ≥ 110/min = +20; systolic blood pressure &lt; 100 mmHg = +30; respiratory rate ≥ 30/min = +20; temperature &lt; 36°C = +20; altered mental status [disorientation, lethargy, stupor, or coma] = +60; arterial oxygen saturation &lt; 90% = +20).\n- Absence of any characteristic =&gt; score of 0 (zero).\n\nThe sum of the 11 points assigned is used to categorize patients into risk classes and associated 30-day mortality:\n- Class I: score ≤ 65, Very low risk, 30-day mortality 0.0-1.6%\n- Class II: score 66-85, Low risk, 30-day mortality 1.7-3.5%\n- Class III: score 86-105, Intermediate risk, 30-day mortality 3.2-7.1%\n- Class IV: score 106-125, High risk, 30-day mortality 4.0-11.4%\n- Class V: score &gt; 125, Very high risk, 30-day mortality 10.0-24.5%",
        "misuse" : "Not to be used to make a diagnosis of pulmonary embolism, as PESI is only meant for risk stratification of pulmonary embolism after the diagnosis has been made.",
        "copyright" : "© Cambio CDS"
      }
    },
    "other_details" : {
      "en" : "Pulmonary embolism severity index (PESI) is a risk-scoring system used to predict the 30-day outcome (mortality and long-term morbidity) in individuals with pulmonary embolism (PE) using 11 non-laboratory based characteristics.",
      "references" : "Aujesky D, Obrosky DS, Stone RA, Auble TE, Perrier A, Cornuz J, Roy PM, Fine MJ. Derivation and validation of a prognostic model for pulmonary embolism. American journal of respiratory and critical care medicine. 2005 Oct 15;172(8):1041-6.\n\nDonzé J, Le Gal G, Fine MJ, Roy PM, Sanchez O, Verschuren F, Cornuz J, Meyer G, Perrier A, Righini M, Aujesky D. Prospective validation of the Pulmonary Embolism Severity Index-A clinical prognostic model for pulmonary embolism. Thrombosis and haemostasis. 2008;100(5):943-8.\n\nFraga M, Taffé P, Méan M, Hugli O, Witzig S, Waeber G, Aujesky D. The inter-rater reliability of the Pulmonary Embolism Severity Index. Thrombosis & Haemostasis. 2010 Dec 10;104(6):1258."
    }
  },
